{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793798777,"nonce":"ouaHOMmNuctYnPHbC9K0TVZePoQ=","message":"LuaJIT trace comparison on de05abe979c2142cdb4271d3ece10907919c23d8 (read-only investigation).\n\nFresh NUC measurements, frozen binaries, alternating arms, taskset 0,2,4,6, ZYLUA_LLVM=1, no diagnostic dumps in timing runs. Exclusive /tmp/zyntax-nuc.lock; cooldown and per-sample temperature/load gates; no cargo/rustc before or after samples; zero throttle-counter changes in all 64 samples. All outputs result: 5.\n\n100,000 iterations, 21 samples per runtime: zylua median wall 30.781 ms / timed region 3.651 ms; LuaJIT 6.132 / 4.298 ms.\n1,000,000 iterations (only n changed), 11 samples per runtime: zylua 57.160 / 28.719 ms; LuaJIT 17.359 / 15.427 ms.\n\nThe short timed-region win is not a steady-state win. LuaJIT records/compiles traces inside that timer; zylua's static-hot whole-chunk compilation happens before os.clock starts. LuaJIT wins the longer timed region by 1.86x and standard wall by 5.02x. The two sizes are not an isolated steady-state slope measurement, but they expose both startup and loop-quality gaps.\n\nActual NUC x86 trace and code findings:\n- LuaJIT trace 5 (Toggle loop at line 43) guards the receiver, metatable/__index lookup, actual method function identities and boolean state in its pre-roll. Method calls inline. Ten activate/value pairs simplify away; the repeated machine loop is two dword stores of the true TValue tag/payload, add, cmp, jle (five instructions). The entire trace including pre-roll/guards is 494 bytes.\n- NthToggle is less ideal: integer count arithmetic and guarded paths form a root trace and side traces. Traces 8/9/10 are 829/1127/1138 bytes and side traces link back to 8. There are loop-unroll-limit recording aborts and interpreter-linked traces during warm-up. Do not describe the entire benchmark as a single five-instruction loop.\n- zylua's optimized HIR and final Cranelift VCODE still carry ten state load/xor/store sequences in the first loop, interleaved with repeated zl_line stores (caller lines 44..53 and accessor line 9), plus a loop-invariant pending-error branch and tier/OSR polling. This is present after Cranelift compilation, not just in input CLIF.\n- HIR has many separate SSA values for the same zl_line global (e.g. v18 and v91..v100 all Global(HirId(1752))). Any memory analysis must canonicalize global storage identity, not assume different SSA roots mean different globals.\n\nRelevant compiler gaps: load_cse.rs reuses loaded values but does not insert a stored value into its available-value map; its provably_disjoint predicate does not use the global-versus-object provenance that licm::may_alias uses. dead_store.rs is intentionally limited to fresh allocations in the same block, excluding this loop-carried object. This explains missing HIR capabilities; the precise contribution of aliasing versus line-store observability needs a controlled implementation experiment.\n\nNext concrete lever: canonical memory identities and typed store-to-load forwarding across single-predecessor success paths, then dead-store/boolean simplification. Preserve widths, volatile/atomic barriers, calls, aliases, cold exits and source locations. Follow with source-position materialization at observable error/debug boundaries and guarded loop peeling for invariant checks. Do not drop line bookkeeping globally, assume arbitrary pointer uniqueness, or disable interpreter-first policy to manufacture a result. Smaller HIR should help both hot code and chunk compilation latency; measure before claiming either.\n\nLuaJIT source corroborates the mechanism: https://github.com/LuaJIT/LuaJIT/blob/v2.1/src/lj_opt_mem.c (semantic alias analysis, store/load forwarding, dead-store elimination); https://github.com/LuaJIT/LuaJIT/blob/v2.1/src/lj_opt_loop.c (pre-roll/copy-substitution, invariant guard elimination and forwarding across loop iterations); https://github.com/LuaJIT/LuaJIT/blob/v2.1/src/lj_snap.c (state restoration for exits). Related Python OSR field-promotion issue: a5de1147fe9cdcbf0d05fa9d1c14e1ec7e0ed88dda766714f47e1a8b3ba6b9d3.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-luajit-method/evidence.tar.gz, SHA256 27a79d99c451cc90f4dcbd14ad7ab2d9fa2142ceb2dba6dd59a77068c954698d. Includes scripts, scores, thermal records, versions/hashes, HIR, CLIF, final VCODE and LuaJIT dumps. zylua binary SHA256 0aabb53d13ed388ab500514943fd8896b6239801bf98c35d2bf421b1731432f8; LuaJIT 2.1.1788856981. Snapshot loading is active in the diagnostic trace. Dump-instrumented compile durations are not used as benchmark timings. No production source changes, builds, or corpus runs in this investigation; issue remains open.","files":null}]}